  "worked_solution": "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to be equal to $2,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $2.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}+2x+b.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+2x+b) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+2(1)+b \\\\  & =1+2+b \\\\  & =b+3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =2 \\\\ b+3 & =2 \\\\ b & =2-3 \\\\ b & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}+cx+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({x}^{2}+cx+2) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+c(1)+2 \\\\  & =1+c+2 \\\\  & =c+3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =2 \\\\ c+3 & =2 \\\\ c & =2-3 \\\\ c & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
